# What each composer removed from a composed layer, per quantity. One row per (composition,
# composed layer, quantity): the amount taken out because two parts counted it twice, the typed
# reason it could not be sized, or the identity that computes it.
#
# The only entry that makes a number smaller
#   An elimination is the only entry in this model that makes a number smaller, and this relation
#   is what the rest of this directory is built on. `composition/fused.sqlc` and
#   `composition/composed_quantities.sqlc` both read it rather than write the join into
#   `pm.elimination` (composition, composed layer, quantity) themselves.
#
# No zero is filled in
#   Reading a missing figure as zero says "nothing was eliminated", which is true only after
#   `composition/owed_equality.sqlc` has removed the fusions that owe no sum. A composer who says
#   "these hours are double counted and I cannot say by how much" files `absent = 'unmeasured'`,
#   and a zero there asserts a reconciliation they declined to make. The blank is carried, and the
#   caller decides.
#
# `quantity` is a column
#   An elimination is per quantity. Demand may be double counted while the nameplate is not: two
#   teams sharing one machine overlap in what they ask of it and not in what it can make. Keeping
#   only `'demand'` is a caller's question, not this relation's.
#
# `claim_seq` is how an elimination reaches its own edge
#   A claim's `narrowsWhen` and `boundOrigin` are keyed on the claim's position in the document,
#   and this table is keyed on a fusion and a quantity, so the position is the only join between an
#   eliminated figure and what its filer said about who set its bound. It is NULL exactly where the
#   quantity is not a claim. It is carried here rather than looked up by a reader, because the
#   figures cannot recover it: two eliminations in one filing may hold the same three values in the
#   same unit.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:eliminations/asrt:elimination, per composed layer and quantity.
SELECT e.composition, e.composed_layer, e.quantity,
       e.low, e.mode, e.high, e.unit,
       e.absent, e.derivation, e.reason, e.claim_seq
FROM pm.elimination e
